  "nameStory": "The lilac bolete gets its charming name from the subtle lilac or purplish tones that can appear on its cap, especially when young or in certain lighting conditions. This delicate coloring sets it apart from its more earthily-colored bolete cousins and makes it a favorite among mushroom enthusiasts who appreciate nature's subtle palette.",
  "description": "The lilac bolete is a medium-sized mushroom with a classic bolete appearance - a rounded cap sitting atop a sturdy stem like a dinner roll on a thick straw. Instead of gills underneath, it has tiny pores that look like a natural sponge, which is the trademark feature of all boletes. The cap can range from brownish to grayish with those distinctive lilac hints, and the whole mushroom has a solid, substantial feel when you pick it up.",
  "idTips": [
    {
      "label": "Cap",
      "description": "Medium brown to grayish-brown cap with subtle lilac or purplish tones, especially visible in younger specimens. Feels smooth and slightly velvety to the touch when dry.",
      "isPrimary": true
    },
    {
      "label": "Pores",
      "description": "Tiny round pores underneath the cap instead of gills, white when young, becoming yellowish with age. Feel spongy and soft when pressed gently.",
      "isPrimary": false
    },
    {
      "label": "Stem",
      "description": "Thick, solid stem that's often club-shaped or swollen at the base. Usually paler than the cap with a firm, meaty texture.",
      "isPrimary": false
    },
    {
      "label": "Spore Print",
      "description": "Olive-brown spore print, which you can get by placing the cap pore-side down on white paper overnight.",
      "isPrimary": false
    },
    {
      "label": "Smell",
      "description": "Pleasant, mild mushroomy smell - not fishy, sour, or unpleasant. Some describe it as slightly nutty or earthy.",
      "isPrimary": false
    },
    {
      "label": "Bruising",
      "description": "May show slight color changes when cut or bruised, but reactions are typically mild compared to other boletes.",
      "isPrimary": false
    }
  ],
  "habitat": "Look for lilac boletes in mixed hardwood and coniferous forests throughout New England, particularly where oak, maple, and pine trees grow together. They form beneficial partnerships with tree roots, so you'll find them on the forest floor near their host trees rather than on decaying wood. They prefer well-drained woodland soils and are often found along forest paths and clearings.",
  "seasonality": {
    "spring": "No mushrooms present, but the underground fungal network is beginning to stir as soil temperatures warm. Look for other early spring fungi instead.",
    "summer": "Prime fruiting season begins in mid to late summer, especially after warm rain showers. Fresh specimens will show the best lilac coloring.",
    "fall": "Peak season continues through early fall, with mushrooms appearing regularly after September rains. Older specimens may lose some of their distinctive coloring.",
    "winter": "Mushroom season ends, though you might occasionally spot dried, weathered remains from the previous season stuck to the forest floor."
  },
  "funFact": "Like all boletes, the lilac bolete is essentially farming underground - its fungal threads connect with tree roots in a partnership so efficient that some trees actually can't survive without their bolete partners, trading sugar for minerals in nature's original cooperative economy!",
  "confusionNotes": "Most likely to be confused with other brown-capped boletes in the same genus, but the lilac bolete's subtle purple tones are the key giveaway. Memory trick: 'Lilac bolete shows its name' - if you can't see any hint of purple or lilac coloring, especially in good light, you're probably looking at a different species.",
  "contextualKnowledge": "The species name 'separans' means 'separating' in Latin, likely referring to how this species was separated or distinguished from other similar boletes when it was first scientifically described. Bolete identification can be tricky even for experts, as many species in this group look quite similar.",
  "compareHints": "Unlike the famous King Bolete which has a more distinctly white pore surface and reddish-brown cap, the lilac bolete shows those characteristic purplish tones and tends to be smaller overall. It also lacks the strong blue bruising reaction seen in some of its relatives like the Curry Bolete, making it one of the gentler-reacting members of the genus. The butter-foot bolete can be distinguished by its bright yellow stem base, which the lilac bolete lacks.",
